.TH HD-DASHBOARD 7 "October 2026" "hd-dashboard" "HyprDebian"
.SH NAME
hd-dashboard \- the hyprdebian dashboard
.SH DESCRIPTION
The package
.B hd-dashboard
turns a hyprdebian system into the hyprdebian dashboard, which shows the state
of every hyprdebian system on one page.
Each system with the package
.B hd-report
sends a status report to the dashboard every 15 minutes, see
.BR hd-report (8).
The systems only make outgoing connections, so no system needs an open port
for it.
.PP
The dashboard keeps the latest report of every system, and a history of the
load, the memory and the fullest pool.
It judges each system on its last report and colors its card by its worst
issue.
.PP
Installing the package sets everything up:
.IP \(bu 2
the default settings, in the key
.B hd-dashboard
of
.IR /etc/hyprdebian/config.json ,
when there are none yet;
.IP \(bu 2
.BR hd-dashboard.service ,
which is enabled and started, and makes the database on its first start.
.PP
The dashboard listens on port 80 by default.
The installation does not open that port in the firewall; that is up to the
admin.
With
.BR ufw (8):
.PP
.RS
sudo ufw allow 80/tcp
.RE
.PP
Then the dashboard answers on
.IR http://<host>/ .
.SH THE SERVICE
.B hd-dashboard.service
runs
.BR "hd-dashboard serve" ,
see
.BR hd-dashboard (8),
as a user that systemd makes up, which may bind to port 80 without being root
and may only write
.IR /var/lib/hd-dashboard .
It is enabled on the first installation; after that, it stays as the admin
left it.
An upgrade restarts it when it is enabled.
Only refused reports and errors get a line in its journal:
.PP
.RS
journalctl \-u hd-dashboard.service
.RE
.PP
.B hd-repo
also uses port 80; give the dashboard another port to have both on one system.
.SH SETTINGS
The settings are the key
.B hd-dashboard
of
.IR /etc/hyprdebian/config.json ,
see
.BR hd-config (7).
A setting that is missing has its default value, from
.IR /usr/share/hd-dashboard/defaults.json .
Restart the service after a change:
.PP
.RS
.nf
sudo hd-config-update hd-dashboard \e
    '{"listen": "0.0.0.0", "port": 8080, "stale_minutes": 45, "offline_days": 14, "history_days": 30}'
sudo systemctl restart hd-dashboard.service
.fi
.RE
.TP
.B listen
The address to listen on, default
.BR 0.0.0.0 .
.TP
.B port
The port to listen on, default 80.
Allow it in the firewall.
.TP
.B stale_minutes
A system that did not report for this many minutes is offline, which is a
note, default 45.
.TP
.B offline_days
A system that did not report for this many days is an error, default 14, so
a laptop that is off for a while does not turn red.
.TP
.B history_days
How many days the history is kept, default 30.
.SH PAGES
.TP
.B POST /report
Stores a report.
.TP
.B GET /
The overview: a card per system, the worst first.
Refreshes every minute.
.TP
.B GET /host/<name>
One system, with the charts of its history and its last report.
.TP
.B GET /api/hosts
The systems, their reports and issues as JSON.
.PP
The connection is plain HTTP, meant for the local network.
.SH REPORTS
A system POSTs its report to
.I /report
as a JSON object, at most 256 KiB, with its token in the header
.BR "Authorization: Bearer <token>" .
The token is 16 to 128 letters and digits.
The report has a
.B format
number, now 1; reports with a newer format are refused.
.B host
is the host name, by which the dashboard knows the system.
The other parts are
.BR fqdn ,
.BR hyprdebian ,
.BR os ,
.BR load ,
.BR cpus ,
.BR memory ,
.BR pools ,
.BR arrays ,
.BR units ,
.BR updates ,
.B snapshot
and
.BR timers ;
the documentation in the repository describes them.
.PP
The first report of a host name registers its token.
Later reports of that host name must have the same token, otherwise they are
refused with 403.
Only a hash of the token is stored.
A reinstalled system has a new token: forget the old one with
.BR "hd-dashboard forget" ,
and the next report registers the new one.
.SH ISSUES
Errors: no report for longer than
.BR offline_days ,
failed units, a pool that is not ONLINE or 90% full or more, errors found by
the last completed scrub of a pool, a degraded mdadm
array, a failed last run of the service of a timer, and Secure Boot that is
not on or not reported, see
.BR hd-secboot-check (8).
.PP
Warnings: a pool that is 80% full or more, bytes repaired by the last
completed scrub of a pool, a system that is starting or
stopping, a pending reboot, no upgrade for 14 days or never, and a HyprDebian
version older than the one on the system of the dashboard, from
.IR /etc/hyprdebian/version.json .
A newer version gets no warning; without that file, versions are not judged.
.PP
Notes: a system that did not report for longer than
.BR stale_minutes ,
and upgradable packages.
Notes do not change the color of the card.
.SH REMOVAL
Removing the package stops the service and keeps the settings and the
database.
Purging it also removes the key
.BR hd-dashboard
and the database.
Firewall rules for the dashboard stay; remove them by hand.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/sbin/hd-dashboard
The dashboard.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/systemd/system/hd-dashboard.service
Runs the dashboard.
.TP
.I /usr/share/hd-dashboard/defaults.json
The default settings.
.TP
.I /etc/hyprdebian/config.json
The settings, in the key
.BR hd-dashboard .
.TP
.I /etc/hyprdebian/version.json
The version of HyprDebian on this system, installed by hd-base.
Hosts with an older version get a warning.
.TP
.I /var/lib/hd-dashboard/dashboard.db
The SQLite database, kept by systemd in
.IR /var/lib/private/hd-dashboard .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR hd-dashboard (8),
.BR hd-report (8),
.BR hd-secboot-check (8),
.BR hd-config (7),
.BR hd-config-update (8),
.BR ufw (8),
.BR systemd.exec (5)
